\chapter{Getting Started: Build, Install, First Programs}
\label{ch:getting-started}

\section{What \DSLtk{} Is}

\DSLtk{} is a toolkit for *building* domain-specific languages, not a
library for *using* one. That distinction matters, because it determines
everything else about the project: the size of the artefact, the shape of its
API, and the amount of ceremony a new language costs you to stand up. The
entire C++ implementation of \DSLtk{} is one file, \code{DSLtk.hpp}, which is
12,024 lines long. The D implementation, \code{DSLtk.d}, is one module of
3,904 lines. There is no separate source file to compile, no static library to
build, no shared object to install, and no code generation step. To use
\DSLtk{} you copy one header into your include tree and you are done.

The implementation targets \cstd{}. This is not a stylistic preference. The
central trick of the library -- passing a string literal as a template
argument, so that \code{dsl::leaf<"num">} produces a tree node whose tag is
known to the compiler -- requires class-type non-type template parameters,
which were only standardised in C++20. The library also leans on concepts in
real constraints and in \code{requires}-expressions, on \code{constexpr}
lambdas, and on the brace of standard-library headers that accompany the
modern language. A toolchain that advertises C++20 support will do; one that
does not will not even parse the header.

The library has no third-party dependencies. This is worth stating precisely,
because it is unusual for a project of this size. The header embeds the
complete public headers of \code{magic_enum} version 0.9.8 between the marker
comments \code{BEGIN DSLTK EMBEDDED MAGIC_ENUM} at line 403 and
\code{END DSLTK EMBEDDED MAGIC_ENUM} at line 5816. Those roughly 5,400 lines of
vendored code provide the enum-reflection machinery, and \DSLtk{} re-exports
the whole of it under the \code{dsl} namespace at lines 5826 through 5870.
There is therefore no \code{magic_enum} include to add, no \code{find_package}
to write, and no version skew to manage: \code{dsl::enum_name},
\code{dsl::enum_cast}, \code{dsl::enum_entries} and the rest work the
moment you include \code{DSLtk.hpp}, because they are already inside it.

The only optional external dependency in the whole project is Lua, and even
that is opt-in twice over: you must define the macro \code{DSLTK_ENABLE_LUA}
before including the header, and you must supply the Lua headers and link the
Lua library yourself. Section~\ref{sec:lua-bridge} covers that.

\section{A First Program}

The smallest useful \DSLtk{} program is a \code{struct} that inherits from
\code{dsl::DSL<Derived, Features...>} and names the features it wants. The
template's first parameter is the deriving type itself -- the curiously
recurring template pattern -- and the remaining parameters are feature tags.
Listing~\ref{lst:ch02-first} is a complete, compilable program that uses two
features: \code{dsl::Pipeline}, which contributes a static \code{wrap} factory
for lifting a value into a chain of transformations, and \code{dsl::Operators},
which contributes a static \code{make_pred} factory for turning a lambda into
a named predicate.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A first \DSLtk{} program: a \code{MathDSL} built from the \code{Pipeline} and \code{Operators} features.},label={lst:ch02-first}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>

struct MathDSL : dsl::DSL<MathDSL, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::Operators> {};

int main () {
  auto total = MathDSL::wrap (5)
             | dsl::pipe ([] (int x) { return x + 3; })
             | dsl::pipe ([] (int x) { return x * 2; });

  auto positive = MathDSL::make_pred ([] (int x) { return x > 0; });

  std::cout << "total = " << total << "\n";
  std::cout << "positive(total) = " << (positive (total) ? "yes" : "no") << "\n";
}
\end{lstlisting}

Compiling and running it produces

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch02-first}.},label={lst:ch02-first-out}]
total = 16
positive(total) = yes
\end{lstlisting}

Three things are worth noticing. First, the pipeline is written with the
ordinary \code{|} operator, and the stages are ordinary lambdas; \DSLtk{}
supplies the plumbing, not a new syntax. Second, the value never leaves the
integer domain: \code{wrap} returns the value unchanged and each \code{pipe}
stage produces a new value, so the chain reads left to right and evaluates
right to left exactly as a hand-written fold would. Third, the DSL type
itself is empty. \code{MathDSL} declares no data members and no member
functions; everything it offers comes from the features it names. This is the
central design claim of the library, and Chapter~\ref{ch:crtp-foundation}
takes it apart in detail.

\section{Obtaining and Installing the Library}

\subsection{Copying the Header}

Because \DSLtk{} is header-only, installation can be as simple as copying
\code{DSLtk.hpp} into your project's include directory. A typical layout is
a vendored \code{third_party/DSLtk/DSLtk.hpp} plus a single include path
entry. There is nothing to link against and nothing to build. The cost of the
library is paid once per translation unit that includes the header, at compile
time; there is no link step and no runtime library.

The header begins with a \code{#pragma once} guard at line 374, so double
inclusion within a translation unit is free. The standard-library headers it
pulls in (twenty-one of them, lines 376 through 397) have their own include
guards.

\subsection{Building with \code{g++} Directly}

The fastest way to check that a toolchain is usable is to compile a small
program with \code{-std=c++20} and nothing else:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Verifying that the header compiles with a bare compiler invocation. The \code{-I} flag points at the directory that holds \code{DSLtk.hpp}.},label={lst:ch02-verify}]
g++ -std=c++20 -I DSLtk example.cpp -o example
./example
\end{lstlisting}

The \code{-std=c++20} flag is not optional. If you omit it, the compiler
defaults to an older dialect and the first error you will see mentions
\code{concept}, \code{requires} or \code{std::same_as}, because the library
uses all three. If you add the flag and still see an error about
\code{DSLtk.hpp: No such file or directory}, the problem is the include path
rather than the standard.

\subsection{The CMake Package}

The project ships a CMake package. \code{DSLtk/CMakeLists.txt} declares a
single \code{INTERFACE} library target named \code{DSLtk}:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={The \code{INTERFACE} target and its usage requirements, from \code{DSLtk/CMakeLists.txt} lines 5--14.},label={lst:ch02-cmake-target}]
add_library(DSLtk INTERFACE)

target_include_directories(DSLtk
    INTERFACE
        $<BUILD_INTERFACE:${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}>
        $<BUILD_INTERFACE:${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/..>
        $<INSTALL_INTERFACE:include>
)

target_compile_features(DSLtk INTERFACE cxx_std_20)
\end{lstlisting}

An \code{INTERFACE} target carries no build rules of its own. It exists to
propagate usage requirements -- here, an include directory and the
\code{cxx_std_20} compile feature -- to whatever links against it. Because
the requirement is expressed as a compile feature rather than a raw flag, a
consumer that already compiles as C++23 or C++26 will keep its own dialect:
CMake treats \code{cxx_std_20} as a minimum, not an override.

The install rules copy \code{DSLtk.hpp} and \code{DSLtk.d} into the include
directory, and export the target into a package configuration under
\code{lib/cmake/DSLtk} together with a \code{pkg-config} file. The intended
workflow is therefore

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={The intended configure, build and install sequence. See the caveat in Section~\ref{sec:cmake-caveat}.},label={lst:ch02-cmake}]
cmake -S DSLtk -B build
cmake --build build
cmake --install build --prefix /usr/local
\end{lstlisting}

followed, in a consuming project, by

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Consuming the installed package.},label={lst:ch02-consume}]
find_package(DSLtk REQUIRED)
target_link_libraries(my_app PRIVATE DSLtk::DSLtk)
\end{lstlisting}

The exported target is namespaced \code{DSLtk::DSLtk}, the conventional
spelling for a target exported from a package named \code{DSLtk}. After the
install, the header sits at \code{include/DSLtk.hpp} and can be included as
\code{#include <DSLtk.hpp>}.

\subsection{A Caveat About the Shipped \code{CMakeLists.txt}}\label{sec:cmake-caveat}

The instructions above describe the design of the CMake package, but the
\code{CMakeLists.txt} as shipped does not configure cleanly on a current
CMake. Three defects prevent the documented sequence from running as written,
and it would be dishonest to present the workflow without them.

First, the file contains no \code{cmake_minimum_required} call. CMake 4
removed compatibility with projects that omit it, so configuration fails
outright with \emph{``No cmake\_minimum\_required command is present''}. The
remedy is a single line at the top of the file, for example
\code{cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.16)}.

Second, the file never includes the \code{GNUInstallDirs} module, yet it uses
\code{CMAKE_INSTALL_INCLUDEDIR} at line 30,
\code{CMAKE_INSTALL_BINDIR} at line 80 and
\code{CMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR} at line 98. All three variables are empty
without that module, so \code{install(...)} is rejected with \emph{``install
FILES given no DESTINATION''}. Adding \code{include(GNUInstallDirs)} resolves
it, and the install then places \code{DSLtk.hpp} and \code{DSLtk.d} under
\code{include/} exactly as intended.

Third, the option \code{DSLtk_BUILD_GENERATOR} defaults to \code{ON} and
builds a parser generator from
\code{generator/DSLtk-ParserGenerator.d}. That source file is not present in
the shipped tree, so the default build fails at the compile step even when the
first two problems are fixed. Passing
\code{-DDSLtk_BUILD_GENERATOR=OFF} avoids it.

With those three adjustments the package behaves exactly as advertised: the
install lays down \code{include/DSLtk.hpp}, \code{include/DSLtk.d},
\code{lib/cmake/DSLtk/DSLtkConfig.cmake},
\code{lib/cmake/DSLtk/DSLtkTargets.cmake} and \code{lib/pkgconfig/DSLtk.pc},
and a consumer that calls \code{find_package(DSLtk)} and links
\code{DSLtk::DSLtk} compiles and runs against the installed header.

\subsection{The D Build}

The D port is a single module, \code{DSLtk.d}, with no dependency beyond
Phobos. It has been exercised with LDC, DMD and GDC. The module header
documents the command that runs the embedded test suite:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Running the D module's embedded unit tests, as documented in the \code{DSLtk.d} header.},label={lst:ch02-dtest}]
ldc2 -unittest -main DSLtk.d -of=dsltk-test
./dsltk-test
\end{lstlisting}

The output is a single line, \emph{``1 modules passed unittests''}. The DMD
and GDC spellings are \code{dmd -unittest -main DSLtk.d -of=dsltk-test} and
\code{gdc -unittest -main DSLtk.d -o dsltk-test} respectively.

Ordinary D programs import the module and use the \code{DSL!} mixin template,
which is the D counterpart of the C++ CRTP base class:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={A D program that uses the \code{DSL!} mixin. The D port takes D string literals directly as template arguments, so \code{FixedString} is optional there.},label={lst:ch02-dhello}]
import DSLtk;
import std.stdio : writeln;

struct BasicDSL { mixin DSL!(Pipeline, Operators); }

void main()
{
    auto result = BasicDSL.wrap(5)
        | pipe((int x) => x + 3)
        | pipe((int x) => x * 2);
    writeln(result);
}
\end{lstlisting}

Compiled with \code{ldc2 -I/path/to/DSLtk hello.d DSLtk.d -of=hello}, it
prints \code{16} -- the same value as the C++ program in
Listing~\ref{lst:ch02-first}, which is the first sign that the two ports are
kept in step. Chapter~\ref{ch:d-toolkit} covers the D composition mechanism
in full; Chapter~\ref{ch:architecture} compares it with the C++ one.

\section{Diagnosing the First Build Failure}

Almost every first-time failure with \DSLtk{} has one of three causes, and
it is worth having them in mind before the first compile.

\paragraph{The wrong language standard.}
If the diagnostic mentions \code{concept}, \code{requires} or
\code{std::same_as}, the compiler is not in C++20 mode. The flag is
\code{-std=c++20} for GCC and Clang, \code{/std:c++20} for MSVC, and
\code{-std=c++2a} for older GCC releases that predate the final name.

\paragraph{The wrong include path.}
If the compiler reports that it cannot find \code{DSLtk.hpp}, the header is
not where the compiler is looking. Check the \code{-I} flag, or, with
CMake, check that the target actually links the \code{DSLtk} interface
target. This is the single most common mistake in a CMake project: adding
\code{include_directories} for a target that links \code{DSLtk::DSLtk}
does not help, because the include directory is a usage requirement of the
interface target and is inherited only by targets that link it.

\paragraph{A compiler without class-type non-type template parameters.}
If the standard is right and the include path is right, and the diagnostic
mentions \code{DSLtk.hpp} and a template argument that looks like a string,
the toolchain does not implement C++20's class-type non-type template
parameters. This is a real possibility: some compilers advertised C++20
support before the class-type NTTP feature was finished. The diagnostic is
characteristically ugly, and the fix is to upgrade the compiler rather than
to work around the library.

There is one further difficulty that is not a fault but a consequence of the
design. Because \DSLtk{} is template-heavy, a mistake in your own code
produces a wall of instantiation traceback rather than a single tidy
message. Two habits help. Build the smallest program that reproduces the
problem, away from the rest of your code; the bundled examples are a good
baseline for this. And read the \emph{first} error in the output rather
than the last: the first is the cause, and everything after it is
cascade.

