Twenty-five of the twenty-six C++ examples compile and run against the
shipped header on GCC 16 and Clang 22. The exception is
\code{21-task-pipeline.cpp}, and the reason is instructive rather than
merely a bug in the example. The example declares
\code{using Result = dsl::Result<std::string, std::string>}, but
\code{dsl::Result<T, E>} stores its payload in a \code{std::variant<T, E>} and
guards its error constructor with \code{requires (!std::same_as<T, E>)}
(line 7550). A variant may contain repeated alternatives, and the indexed
\code{from_ok}/\code{from_err} factories can construct this result. Its
type-based queries cannot select a unique alternative, however, so normal
result inspection fails to instantiate. Distinct payload types avoid that
defect in application results. The shipped task types hard-code the
duplicate-type specialization; Chapter~\ref{ch:task-pipeline} explains the
additional task-state constraints.

\section{The Three-Tier Wrapper Idiom}

The header's own quick-start comment recommends a three-tier arrangement, and
it is the arrangement this book assumes throughout. The bottom tier is the
toolkit itself, \code{DSLtk.hpp}, which knows nothing about your domain. The
middle tier is a wrapper header, conventionally \code{mydsl.hpp}, which
includes the toolkit and defines your DSL type along with any domain-specific
vocabulary. The top tier is your application, which includes only the wrapper
header and never mentions \DSLtk{} at all.

Figure~\ref{fig:ch02-three-tier} draws the arrangement and the include
edges that connect it.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\begin{dslgraph}
  \node[dsllarge] (toolkit) {Tier 1\\\code{DSLtk.hpp}\\the toolkit: features,\\value types, free functions};
  \node[dsllarge, below=13mm of toolkit] (wrapper) {Tier 2\\\code{mydsl.hpp}\\the wrapper header: includes\\the toolkit, defines\\your DSL type};
  \node[dsllarge, below=13mm of wrapper] (app) {Tier 3\\\code{main.cpp}, \code{lib/*.cpp}\\the application: includes\\only \code{mydsl.hpp}};

  \draw[dsledge] (toolkit) -- node[right, font=\scriptsize, align=left] {\texttt{\#include "DSLtk.hpp"}:\\(toolkit into wrapper)} (wrapper);
  \draw[dsledge] (wrapper) -- node[right, font=\scriptsize, align=left] {\texttt{\#include "mydsl.hpp"}:\\(wrapper into application)} (app);

  \draw[dslfeedback] (app.east) -- ++(18mm,0) |- node[pos=0.25, right, font=\scriptsize] {never includes} (toolkit.east);
\end{dslgraph}
\caption{The three-tier wrapper arrangement. The toolkit knows nothing of the
domain; the wrapper is the only file that sees both the toolkit and the
domain; the application sees only the wrapper.}
\label{fig:ch02-three-tier}
\end{figure}

The value of this arrangement is not aesthetic. Because the application never
names \code{dsl::} anything, the surface of your language is exactly what you
put in the wrapper, and you can change it without touching a single call
site. Because the toolkit appears in exactly one translation unit's include
graph, its compile-time cost is paid once rather than once per user of your
language. And because the dependency arrow between tier 3 and tier 1 does not
exist, tier 3 cannot accidentally acquire a dependency on \DSLtk{}'s internal
shapes.

The wrapper header in Listing~\ref{lst:ch02-wrapper} is the entire middle
tier for the \code{MathDSL} of Listing~\ref{lst:ch02-first}.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The middle tier. This is the only file in the project that includes \code{DSLtk.hpp}.},label={lst:ch02-wrapper}]
#pragma once

#include "DSLtk.hpp"

namespace maths {

/// The domain language: a pipeline of transformations over integers.
struct MathDSL : dsl::DSL<MathDSL, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::Operators>
{
  /// Lifts an integer into the language.
  static constexpr int
  square (int x)
  {
    return x * x;
  }
};

} // namespace maths
\end{lstlisting}

\section{A Recipe: Wrapping a Raw Lambda as a DSL Feature}

The most common thing a new DSL author wants to do is take a function that
already exists and give it a name that means something in the domain. The
library makes this a two-line exercise, and it is worth spelling the recipe
out because it recurs throughout the book.

The recipe has four steps: name the operation, choose the layer at which it
should live, wrap the implementation, and expose it. The procedure below states the recipe.

\begin{pseudocode}[Wrapping a raw lambda as a DSL feature]
\textbf{procedure} wrap\_as\_feature (name, layer, lambda)\\
\quad \textbf{if} layer = \code{namespace} then\\
\qquad \textbf{return} free function \code{name} delegating to lambda\\
\quad \textbf{if} layer = \code{mixin} then\\
\qquad define feature tag \code{Tag}\\
\qquad define nested \code{Mixin<Derived>} for \code{Tag}\\
\qquad \qquad add member forwarding to lambda\\
\qquad \textbf{return} tag \code{Tag}\\
\quad \textbf{if} layer = \code{member} then\\
\qquad \textbf{return} member function of \code{Derived} delegating to lambda
\end{pseudocode}

The choice of layer is the interesting part. A \emph{namespace-scope} free
function is the default and the most common: it lives in \code{dsl} (or in
your own namespace) and is reachable from anywhere. A \emph{mixin} member is
appropriate when the operation is only meaningful for a particular DSL, or
when it needs to inject an operator that must not leak into the global
namespace. A \emph{member} of the derived class is appropriate when the
operation is intrinsic to the domain and should read as part of the language's
vocabulary.

For example, the pipeline stage in Listing~\ref{lst:ch02-first} can be
promoted to a named domain operation with no more than the code in
Listing~\ref{lst:ch02-stage}. The stage remains an ordinary lambda wrapped in
\code{dsl::pipe}; what changes is that the domain now has a word for it.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A named pipeline stage, continuing the \code{maths::MathDSL} of Listing~\ref{lst:ch02-wrapper}. The lambda is unchanged; only its packaging is.},label={lst:ch02-stage}]
namespace maths {

/// A named stage: doubling a value.
inline auto
double_it ()
{
  return dsl::pipe ([] (int x) { return x * 2; });
}

} // namespace maths

auto total = maths::MathDSL::wrap (5)
           | maths::double_it ()
           | dsl::pipe ([] (int x) { return x + 3; });
\end{lstlisting}

Chapter~\ref{ch:feature-tags} develops this recipe into the general
mechanism, including how to inject an operator through a hidden friend
without polluting the global namespace.

\section{Enum Reflection for Free}

The embedded \code{magic_enum} block means enum reflection is available
without any additional include or link step.
Listing~\ref{lst:ch02-enum} uses it.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Enum reflection with no dependency and no separate include.},label={lst:ch02-enum}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>

enum class Token { Number = -1, Identifier = 2, End = 7 };

int main () {
  static_assert (dsl::enum_count<Token> () == 3);
  static_assert (dsl::enum_name<Token::Identifier> () == "Identifier");

  for (auto [value, name] : dsl::enum_entries<Token> ())
    std::cout << dsl::enum_integer (value) << " -> " << name << "\n";
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch02-enum}.},label={lst:ch02-enum-out}]
-1 -> Number
2 -> Identifier
7 -> End
\end{lstlisting}

Note that the enumerator values need not be contiguous and need not start at
zero; \code{magic_enum} uses compiler introspection while scanning a bounded
configured range, by default minus 128 through 127. The \code{dsl} namespace also re-exports the
\code{containers}, \code{customize}, \code{bitwise_operators},
\code{iostream_operators}, \code{istream_operators} and
\code{ostream_operators} sub-namespaces of \code{magic_enum} at lines
5826--5832, so the whole library is reachable under a single prefix. Chapter
\ref{ch:enum-reflection} covers the details, including the
\code{customize::enum_range} specialisation that turns an enum into a flags
enum.

\section{The Optional Lua Bridge}
\label{sec:lua-bridge}

The preprocessor shipped with \DSLtk{} supports macros, includes, conditionals
and a scripting hook. The scripting hook is where Lua comes in, and it is
strictly optional. Two conditions must both hold before any Lua code is
compiled: the macro \code{DSLTK_ENABLE_LUA} must be defined, and the Lua
headers must be reachable. At line 399 the header guards its Lua include
with exactly that condition:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={The Lua include is conditional on a user-supplied macro.},label={lst:ch02-lua-include}]
#if defined(DSLTK_ENABLE_LUA)
#include <lua.hpp>
#endif
\end{lstlisting}

When the macro is defined, the header additionally defines
\code{dsl::lua_script_hook} at line 11348, a factory that takes a
\code{lua_State *} and returns the \code{std::function} the preprocessor
calls for a \code{#lua} directive. The caller owns the Lua state; the hook
restores the Lua stack top on the way out and translates a script failure into
a \code{std::runtime_error}. Listing~\ref{lst:ch02-lua} wires it up.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Enabling and using the Lua bridge. The macro must be defined before the header is included, and the Lua library must be linked by the caller.},label={lst:ch02-lua}]
#define DSLTK_ENABLE_LUA
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <lua.hpp>

int main ()
{
  lua_State *state = luaL_newstate ();
  luaL_openlibs (state);

  dsl::Preprocessor preprocessor;
  preprocessor.script = dsl::lua_script_hook (state);

  std::cout << preprocessor.process ("#lua return 'answer: ' .. 42\n");

  lua_close (state);
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is \code{answer: 42}. Without the macro the code does not compile
at all, because \code{lua.hpp} is never included and
\code{dsl::lua_script_hook} does not exist; that is the correct behaviour
for a header-only library that must not drag a scripting runtime into every
program that uses it. Chapter~\ref{ch:preprocessor} covers the preprocessor
properly.

\section{Edge Cases and Limits}

The first day of work with \DSLtk{} is usually spent on build problems, so it
is worth being precise about the ones that the shipped code actually
produces.

\paragraph{The language standard is not negotiable.}
\cstd{} is required, and specifically the class-type non-type template
parameter support that C++20 introduced. A toolchain that parses C++20 but
does not implement class-type NTTPs will reject \code{dsl::leaf<"tag">} and
\code{dsl::pattern<"...">}. If you are unsure, compile a two-line program
that instantiates \code{dsl::FixedString<4>{"abc"}} before you blame the
library.

\paragraph{The shipped CMake package does not configure as shipped.}
As set out in Section~\ref{sec:cmake-caveat}, the missing
\code{cmake_minimum_required}, the missing \code{GNUInstallDirs} include and
the absent generator source each break the documented CMake workflow
independently. Until they are fixed, copy the header, or apply the three
corrections yourself.

\paragraph{Predicate composition does not compile.}
\code{dsl::Predicate} provides \code{operator&}, \code{operator|} and
\code{operator!} at lines 6013, 6023 and 6031, and the header's documentation
advertises them. They do not build. Each operator returns
\code{Predicate{ ... }} from inside a member function of \code{Predicate},
where the injected-class-name \code{Predicate} denotes the current
specialisation \code{Predicate<F>} rather than the class template. No
class-template argument deduction therefore takes place, and the generic
lambda that was meant to become the new callable cannot be converted to
\code{F}. The workaround is to compose predicates yourself -- a two-line
lambda -- or to keep the predicates separate and combine their results with
\code{&&}, as Listing~\ref{lst:ch02-first} does. Chapter~\ref{ch:operators}
returns to this in detail.

\paragraph{One bundled example does not compile.}\label{sec:ch02-example-21}
\code{21-task-pipeline.cpp} instantiates \code{dsl::Result<std::string,
std::string>}, whose type-based queries fail to instantiate. See
Chapter~\ref{ch:task-pipeline}.

\paragraph{The header is large and included in full.}
\DSLtk{} is a single 12,024-line header with twenty-one standard-library
includes. Every translation unit that includes it parses all of it, even if
it uses two features. In a large project, the mitigation is the three-tier
arrangement for interface organization. A wrapper still includes the toolkit
in every translation unit that uses it; it does not itself reduce parsing
work. Precompiled headers or a compiled facade can address that cost.
The instantiation-heavy parts
are the expression templates and the parser combinators, because they
generate many distinct types; if compile time becomes a problem, confine the
heavy DSL usage to a small number of translation units.

\paragraph{There is no separate build artefact to debug.}
Because there is no library, there is no ABI to preserve and no version to
match. The cost of that convenience is that every consumer recompiles when
the header changes, and that two translation units compiled against different
versions of \DSLtk{} may disagree about a type. Pin the header in your
version control and upgrade it deliberately.

\section{What Comes Next}

You now have a compiling \DSLtk{} program, a working installation path for
both language ports, and a catalogue of twenty-six small examples that
isolate each facility. Chapter~\ref{ch:architecture} opens the black box:
it maps the physical structure of \code{DSLtk.hpp}, distinguishes the two
kinds of feature the library defines, and lays out the layering that the
remaining chapters follow.
